
I investigated whether marketing performance improved between July–December 2024 and the same period in 2025.

I found that the overall conversion rate increased from 5.71% in 2024 to 5.95% in 2025. When I broke the results down by channel, I saw that all four channels 
improved: email increased from 6.48% to 6.76%, Organic Search from 8.23% to 8.57%, Paid Search from 4.74% to 4.94% and Paid Social from 3.07% to 3.18%. 
I also checked the channel mix and found that it remained almost unchanged between the two years, so the improvement was not caused by a major shift in traffic 
between channels. To account for seasonality, I compared each month with the same month in the previous year and conversion rate was higher in every month 
from July through December. This also showed that the result was not driven by a single seasonal period and I did not find evidence of Simpson's Paradox in this case.

Based on these findings, I would maintain the current channel allocation while focusing on improving conversion efficiency across all channels. 
I would give particular attention to Organic Search and Email because they have the highest conversion rates, while continuing to monitor channel-level performance 
before making any major changes to the marketing budget.
